  "live_records": {
    "q-141824": {
      "dom_id": "step-q141824",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $-6{y}^{2}+24y=21.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}-4y+4$ is a perfect square.*",
      "worked_solution": "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4y+4=(y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{y}^{2}+24y & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-4y) & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y) & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}) & =21 \\\\ -6[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =21 \\\\ -6[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+(-6)(-4) & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+24 & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $24$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(y-2)^{2}+24 & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+24-24 & =21-24 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-6)} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ |y-2| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
