    "q-141806": {
      "dom_id": "step-q141806",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $4{x}^{2}-8x=5.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*",
      "worked_solution": "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-8x & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+4 & =5+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-141806-a",
