    "q-141826": {
      "dom_id": "step-q141826",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $-7{y}^{2}-42y=62.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}+6y+9$ is a perfect square.*",
      "worked_solution": "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+6y+9=(y+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{y}^{2}-42y & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+6y) & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y) & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =62 \\\\ -7[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =62 \\\\ -7[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+(-7)(-9) & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+63 & =62\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $63$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-7),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(y+3)^{2}+63 & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+63-63 & =62-63 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(-7)} \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{7}} \\\\ |y+3| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\ y & =-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
