    "q-60360": {
      "dom_id": "step-q60360",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The equation $2{x}^{2}-4x-9=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x-9 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x & =9 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-2x) & =9 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{(1)}^{2}-{(1)}^{2}] & =9 \\\\ 2[{(x-1)}^{2}-1] & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2}-2 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =9+2 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $q=11.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11 \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-1)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
