    "q-60351": {
      "dom_id": "step-q60351",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "The equation $5{x}^{2}+2x-3=0$ can be written in the form $5(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}x) & =3 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}x+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}] & =3 \\\\ 5[{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{25} & =3 \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5} & =3 \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & =3+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $q=\\frac{16}{5}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=\\frac{16}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{15}{5}=3.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
