    "q-60363": {
      "dom_id": "step-q60363",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "The equation $-4{x}^{2}-6x+10=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-6x+10) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =10 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =10 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =10+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}=-\\frac{10}{4}=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-60363-a",
