    "q-60357": {
      "dom_id": "step-q60357",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "The equation $-5{x}^{2}+5x-1=0$ can be written in the form $5(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-5{x}^{2}+5x-1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-5x & =-1 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}-x) & =-1 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 5[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =-1 \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4} & =-1 \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{4}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
