    "q-86415": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86415",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "The equation $-4{w}^{2}+40w-75=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}+40w-75) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w & =-75 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}-10w) & =-75 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}-2\\cdot 5w+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}] & =-75 \\\\ 4[{(w-5)}^{2}-25] & =-75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2}-4\\cdot 25 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2}-100 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =-75+100 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=25.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w-5)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w-5)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w-5| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w-5 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =5\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =5+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ w & =5-\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-86415-a",
